SWITCH-CASE STATEMENTS

switch-case statements serve a similar function to if statements, with a variation in syntax helping codify everything into 'cases' rather than the broader categories a if statement
is normally meant to handle with its else-if and else categories.

its syntax is:

switch(expression, such as a variable of any kind or an operation)
{
  case a:
    //whatever code you want
    break;  <-- important you append this after every case!
  case b:
    //code
    break;
...
  default:
    //code
    break;
}

  EX:
  short dinglebob = 4;
  switch (dinglebob)
  {
    case 0:
    Console.WriteLine("abcdefg");
    break;
    ...
    case 4:
    Console.WriteLine("That's the bob, alright!");
    break;
  }

